WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:02.638
Checkout still returns a success response.

00:00:02.809 --> 00:00:06.467
But the app changed how much it asked the
payment service to charge.

00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:10.854
Here's how to catch that change with an
outbound HTTP assertion.

00:00:11.419 --> 00:00:13.935
In this demo, the recording calls the

00:00:13.935 --> 00:00:17.130
payment endpoint with an amount of 2,400
cents.

00:00:17.301 --> 00:00:19.880
The changed fixture sends 4,800.

00:00:20.051 --> 00:00:21.916
The checkout response stays the same.

00:00:22.485 --> 00:00:26.029
Add the HTTP Output assertion to your test
config.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:31.250
Use include only to compare calls to the
payment host, and run with recorded mocks

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.206
attached so there's outbound traffic to
compare.

00:00:34.752 --> 00:00:39.648
After analysis, open Accuracy and find
Outbound HTTP calls.

00:00:39.819 --> 00:00:42.151
The Argument row points to the amount
field.

00:00:42.322 --> 00:00:44.120
Expected came from the recording.

00:00:44.291 --> 00:00:47.774
Actual came from the request the app sent
during the replay.

00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:52.644
The local scorer passes the baseline
fixtures and reports one Argument

00:00:52.644 --> 00:00:54.869
difference for the changed fixtures.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.397
On a cloud report, that run-wide difference
puts the replay in Missed Goals.

00:00:59.952 --> 00:01:02.373
Fix the outbound request, rerun the same

00:01:02.373 --> 00:01:05.157
recording, and confirm the difference is
gone.
